            <ArticleTitle>Patients with low back pain demonstrate increased activity of the posterior oblique sling muscle during prone hip extension.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>400-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmrj.2013.12.006</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-1482(13)01227-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine activation patterns of the myofascial chain in women experiencing chronic low back pain (CLBP) and women without CLBP during a prone hip extension (PHE) test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross-sectional.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Clinical research laboratory.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fifteen women experiencing CLBP and 15 women without CLBP.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Surface electromyographic recordings from the posterior oblique sling during PHE.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two-sample t-tests were used to compare demographic information and electromyographic signal amplitude of the posterior oblique sling between groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Women with CLBP exhibited significantly increased normalized electromyographic signal amplitudes in the contralateral latissimus dorsi (P = .01), contralateral elector spinae (P &lt; .01), ipsilateral elector spinae (P &lt; .01), ipsilateral gluteus maximus (P = .03), and ipsilateral biceps femoris (P = .02) compared with women without CLBP.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Women with CLBP had greater activity in the posterior oblique sling muscles than did women without CLBP during PHE. These findings suggest that an alteration can be made in posterior oblique sling muscle activities during PHE in women with CLBP.</AbstractText>
